AI-nime feels like the right name. The art can get more polished and the story can stay gloriously cheesy. Give me a gorgeous rain-soaked duel where the villain takes six full seconds to turn around and say, “You still don’t remember who taught you to hold a sword, do you?” I’m watching the next episode.

And some of the slop could become part of the genre’s vocabulary. Imagine a character’s coat changing color between cuts. Most of the time it’s a continuity error. Then one show does it on purpose, and by episode four you’re rewinding a scene because his coat was red before she said his name.

That feels like a natural fit for Pantheon. Call it Previously, in a Different Life. Every episode opens with a recap of something that never happened on-screen. The first few recaps are pure soap opera. Then one morning the protagonist wakes up beside a woman who remembers twenty years of marriage, and he recognizes her from the recap.